# Benchmarks, 2026-11-26: `cig run` on the VM, and a VM that got faster under the native code

<!-- SPDX-License-Identifier: CC-BY-4.0 -->

The 25 programs of [`bench/compute`](../bench/compute/), each written in CigScript and again in Python (all 25), JavaScript (24: all but `csv`), Lua (21) and Rust (8), timed side by side on one machine, fifteen hours after [the run of 2026-11-25](BENCHMARKS-2026-11-25.md): this one began at 20:02 UTC on the 25th, which was the 26th where it was made, and is named for that. Four things changed between the two runs, and this run measures them:

- **`cig run` runs a script on the VM.** The default engine is `auto` ([#259](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-script/issues/259), cig-script#285 and #287): the tree-walker runs a script's first two runs, the second leaves the lowering to a process of its own, and from the third the VM runs the script from the module it kept. `cig run` in this paper is that third run and every one after it: each program was run twice before the rounds. In the run of 2026-11-25 `cig run` was the tree-walker, which is `cig run --engine tree` here.
- **The VM's fast loop counts a loop turn's steps in one instruction**, where a turn had two, and counting is a subtraction and a branch (cig-script#280 and #286). Steps are counted whether or not a run has a step budget; these runs have none.
- **The VM's fast loop runs `for x in xs`** (cig-script#286). A turn of a loop over a list took the VM's general path; it stays in the fast loop now.
- **Stage `c` frees a loop's scratch registers once**, where the loop is entered, and writes them with a plain store from then on, where every write tested the register first ([cig-compile#115](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-compile/pull/115)).

**This is a run of cig's source, not of a release.** None of it is in a release yet, so cig is built here from commit `ca5236b` and cigcompile from commit `6882f02`, each as its release workflow builds it for Linux (`cargo build --release --target x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`). cigcompile at that commit lowers a script with cig-script at `da5cd6f`; between that commit and `ca5236b` the lowering, the bytecode and the VM differ by nothing that runs (names for the value tags and a set-of-tags type were added, which nothing reads yet). It is the machine of the run of 2026-11-25, on the same core, with the same programs and the same builds of Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust, so [one section](#since-the-last-paper) sets the two runs side by side. Within this one, every side ran in the same rounds.

Every number of the comparison is made from the samples in [`bench/results/2026-11-26/`](../bench/results/2026-11-26/) by [`bench/compare/report.py`](../bench/compare/report.py), and made again by [`bench/compare/check.py`](../bench/compare/check.py), a second program written from the [specification](../bench/compare/SPEC.md) alone; the two agree on every one.

## In short

- **`cig run` takes {{geomean.compute.cig.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time** at the geometric mean of the {{n.compute.cig.lua}} compute programs both have, where the tree-walker took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.cig.lua}} in the run before, and {{geomean.compute.cig.python}} of CPython 3.12's, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.cig.python}}: faster than CPython on {{faster.compute.cig.python}} of {{n.compute.cig.python}} programs, where it was faster on {{earlier:2026-11-25:faster.compute.cig.python}}. It runs the bytecode a stage `b` program runs, on the same VM: a stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.cig}} of its time.
- **A stage `c` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time**: faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.lua}} of {{n.compute.stage-c.lua}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-c.lua}}. In the run before it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.lua}} times. Against CPython 3.12 it takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.python}} of the time (it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.python}}), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit-off}} times LuaJIT's interpreter's ({{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit-off}}), {{geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit}} times LuaJIT's with its compiler on ({{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.luajit}}), and {{geomean.compute.stage-c.rust}} times Rust's over the {{n.compute.stage-c.rust}} programs Rust has ({{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.rust}}). Node 18 takes {{median.hello.node}} ms to start on this machine, which decides its row ({{geomean.compute.stage-c.node}}).
- **The bytecode alone got faster again.** A stage `b` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}} times Lua 5.4's time, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-b.lua}}, and {{geomean.compute.stage-b.python}} of CPython's, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-b.python}}. `sieve`, which ends by walking a list of 300,001 elements, takes {{median.sieve.stage-b}} ms as a stage `b` program, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.sieve.stage-b}}.
- **Over all {{n.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} compute programs a stage `c` program takes {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-b}} of stage `b`'s time**, as it did in the run before ({{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}): faster on {{faster.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, level (within 3%) on {{level.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}, slower on {{slower.compute.stage-c.stage-b}}. `mandel` takes {{ratio.mandel.stage-c.stage-b}} of its stage `b` time, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.mandel.stage-c.stage-b}}: chiefly, its loops no longer test a register before every write.
- **Three programs come out slower with their native code than as bytecode:** `maps` ({{ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}), `strings` ({{ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}}) and `words` ({{ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}), where in the run before they were level ({{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}}, {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}). Timed alone, `maps` and `words` are {{earlier:alone:ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}} and {{earlier:alone:ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}, and `strings` is at the 3% line. Their loops are strings and dictionary lookups, which the runtime does for the native code one call at a time ([below](#stage-c-against-stage-b)).
- **Start-up:** `hello` takes {{median.hello.stage-b}} ms as a stage `b` program, {{median.hello.stage-c}} as a stage `c` one and {{median.hello.cig}} under `cig run`. CPython takes {{median.hello.python}} ms, Lua {{median.hello.lua}} and Node {{median.hello.node}}.

## Since the last paper

Each side's median time in this run divided by its median in [the run of 2026-11-25](BENCHMARKS-2026-11-25.md). Under 1, the side took less time than it did in that run.

{{table:since:2026-11-25}}

- **The other languages are the yardstick.** Lua, LuaJIT, CPython, Node and Rust ran from the same binaries on the same core in both runs, and nothing about them changed: their columns say how far two runs of one thing differ on this machine, and their geometric means are in the last row. What cig's columns show beyond that is cig's.
- **`cig run`** changed engines: its column is the VM in this run over the tree-walker in that one. `trees` takes {{median.trees.cig}} ms, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.trees.cig}}; `mandel` {{median.mandel.cig}}, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.mandel.cig}}. A stage `a` program, which still runs its script on the tree-walker, takes {{geomean.compute.stage-a.lua}} times Lua's time, where it took {{earlier:2026-11-25:geomean.compute.stage-a.lua}}: nothing changed for it.
- **Stage `b`** is the VM's own gains, beyond what the yardstick moved. The two changes to its fast loop landed together, and this run cannot give each its share. By what the programs do: `loop` and `mandel` are loops of arithmetic and little else, where a turn's steps are a good part of the turn, and `sieve` and `words` have the loops over a list.
- **Stage `c`** has the VM's gains where its code is bytecode, and its own on `mandel`, whose loops write their scratch registers with a plain store (cig-compile#115 measured that alone, on one runtime, at 0.66 of `mandel`'s time). `sieve`'s native code walked its list already, so its stage `c` time did not move while its stage `b` time did.

## Stage c against stage b

Each program's median time as a stage `b` program and as a stage `c` program, the second divided by the first, and what of the program cigcompile gave native code: its top level, so many of its functions, or nothing. The last two rows are the geometric mean of the ratios over every compute program and over the ones with native code, with how many were faster, level (within 3%) and slower.

{{table:native}}

**What gets native code.** `cigcompile build --stage c` compiles a script's bytecode loop by loop to machine code that runs on the same registers cig's VM uses, so native code can sit beside anything: what it has no machine code for, the runtime runs.

- **A loop of arithmetic** on ints, floats and bools, with a list's elements read and written by an int: `mandel`, `loop`, `fannkuch`, `sieve`.
- **A loop over a list** (`for x in xs`), and `push`: the list's elements are read where they lie, and an element that owns memory (a string, a list, a record) is put in its register by the runtime.
- **A record's fields.** A map a script writes as a record has a shape, the runtime keeps for each field instruction the shape it last saw and the field's place, and machine code compares one number and reads or writes the value there: `nbody` {{ratio.nbody.stage-c.stage-b}}, `richards` {{ratio.richards.stage-c.stage-b}}.
- **A function on numbers alone**, compiled whole for the argument types it is called with: `fib`.
- **One instruction the runtime runs for it**: a string built or compared, a builtin called, a dictionary read by a computed key. Machine code calls the runtime for that one instruction and goes on. Such a call costs about what the interpreter takes for the instruction, so a loop made of them gains little by being compiled, and since the VM's own loop got faster it can lose: `regex-ish` {{ratio.regex-ish.stage-c.stage-b}}, `lists` {{ratio.lists.stage-c.stage-b}}, `strings` {{ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}}, `maps` {{ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, `words` {{ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}.
- **Not yet:** a loop whose work is calls (`closures`, `dispatch`, `trees`, and the inner loops of `spectral`, whose three functions stay bytecode under a native top level: {{ratio.spectral.stage-c.stage-b}}). A call from native code goes through the VM, which costs more than the interpreter making the call itself, so the compiler leaves such a loop to the bytecode. Nor a type for what a field or an element holds: every `+` on a value read from one still tests its operands.

**Two are slower, and a third is at the line.** `maps`, `strings` and `words` come out {{ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, {{ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}} and {{ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}} of their stage `b` time, past the 3% line, `strings` by a hair. In the run of 2026-11-25 they were {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}} and {{earlier:2026-11-25:ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}. Timed alone for {{earlier:alone:rounds.pass1}} rounds under each of two other seeds ([`alone/`](../bench/results/2026-11-26/alone/)), they come out {{earlier:alone:ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-b}}, {{earlier:alone:ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-b}} and {{earlier:alone:ratio.words.stage-c.stage-b}}, and against the same stage `c` program run without its native code (`CIG_NATIVE=0`) {{earlier:alone:ratio.maps.stage-c.stage-c-off}}, {{earlier:alone:ratio.strings.stage-c.stage-c-off}} and {{earlier:alone:ratio.words.stage-c.stage-c-off}}.

- **`maps` and `words`** are behind their bytecode by 5% or more on both comparisons: that is their native code, not the run.
- **`strings`** is at the line against its stage `b` program and 7% behind itself without its native code. Those two runs of its bytecode differ between themselves ({{earlier:alone:median.strings.stage-b}} ms as a stage `b` program, {{earlier:alone:median.strings.stage-c-off}} as the stage `c` program without its native code), so all this run says of `strings` is that its native code does not help it.
- **What moved between the two runs.** As stage `b` programs the three take {{median.maps.stage-b}}, {{median.strings.stage-b}} and {{median.words.stage-b}} ms, where they took {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.maps.stage-b}}, {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.strings.stage-b}} and {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.words.stage-b}}; as stage `c` programs {{median.maps.stage-c}}, {{median.strings.stage-c}} and {{median.words.stage-c}}, where they took {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.maps.stage-c}}, {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.strings.stage-c}} and {{earlier:2026-11-25:median.words.stage-c}}. `words` got faster as bytecode by a tenth, with its two loops over a list, and by half that with its native code. `maps` and `strings` moved as bytecode by what the other languages moved between the runs, and did not get faster with their native code.
- **Why it can happen.** The compiler plans each loop from figures of what an instruction costs as machine code, as a call of the runtime and in the interpreter, and by those figures a plan is never worse than the bytecode. The figures were measured against the VM as it was before its fast loop changed. What moved these programs is not separated here; it is [cig-compile#117](https://github.com/otmof-ops/cig-compile/issues/117), with measuring the figures again.
- **The short ones.** `json-deep` has no native code: its stage `b` and stage `c` programs are one file, and it comes out at {{ratio.json-deep.stage-c.stage-b}} here and {{earlier:alone:ratio.json-deep.stage-c.stage-b}} alone, which is what two copies of one program of three milliseconds differ by. `deltablue`, whose top level is native, comes out at {{ratio.deltablue.stage-c.stage-b}} here and {{earlier:alone:ratio.deltablue.stage-c.stage-b}} alone.

**What the native code itself adds:** the same stage `c` programs run with `CIG_NATIVE=0`, which makes them run their bytecode and never load their native code, take {{inverse.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}} times as long at the geometric mean (stage `c` ÷ the same program without its native code: {{geomean.compute.stage-c.stage-c-off}}).

## Against each language

A cell is cig's time divided by the other language's: the geometric mean, over the programs both have, of the ratio of their median times. Under 1, cig took less time. In brackets: how many of those programs cig was faster on, level with (within 3%) and slower on.

{{table:ratios-compute}}

- **`cig run`** is `cig run script.cig` as it runs a script from its third run on: on cig's VM, from the module it kept. The tree-walker, which runs a script's first two runs, is `cig run --engine tree` in [cig's own sides](#cigs-own-sides).
- **A stage `a` program** is what `cigcompile build` makes by default: cig itself with the script inside it, run by the tree-walker.
- **A stage `b` program** (`cigcompile build --stage b`) runs the script's bytecode on cig's VM.
- **A stage `c` program** (`cigcompile build --stage c`) is a stage `b` program with native code beside its bytecode.

These are the {{programs.compute}} programs that are not start-up tests; start-up has [its own section](#start-up).

